Chapter: TlsNoAuthLoopbackTest
  cites Encode chapter TlsEndpoint
  cites Encode chapter X509
  cites Encode chapter X509Chain
  cites Foreword chapter Maybe

 A TLS 1.3 handshake completing WITHOUT authentication, and the same
 client refusing to complete when it asked for authentication and did not
 get it. Both halves matter and they are the same code path.

 A client with no trust anchors does not require the server to be
 authenticated. It still cannot verify the certificate, and until this
 test existed that was fatal for the wrong reason: the Certificate and
 CertificateVerify messages were folded into the transcript only when
 they verified, so a client that could not verify them held a transcript
 one message short of the server's and the server Finished never matched.
 The handshake failed at the Finished, which reads like a key schedule
 fault and is nothing of the kind.

 The transcript records what was RECEIVED. Authentication is a separate
 verdict carried by tep-auth-done, and tep-auth-missing is what refuses.

 no-anchors is the case that could not complete before. tampered is the
 control that proves the fix did not buy it by weakening anything: that
 client DOES carry the real anchor, so it requires authentication, and it
 is handed a Certificate with a flipped byte. It must not finish.

